From: sheng-development
Subject: [PATCH] Authenticate with a fingerprint at the greeter

SDDM runs a single PAM conversation, and `pam_fprintd` is `sufficient` and
first in it, so the greeter is blocked for the whole 30 s fprintd timeout
before the password it is already holding gets looked at:

	15:13:33.341  "Place your finger on the fingerprint reader"
	15:14:03.364  "Verification timed out"
	15:14:03.534  Authentication for user "sushy" successful

The 50 ms between the last two lines is the password that was typed at the
start. Every login costs 30 s unless the finger is used, and the Breeze
greeter has no fingerprint code at all, so nothing says the reader is live.

KWin's lock screen does not have this problem because kscreenlocker runs a
second, fingerprint-only PAM service (`kde-fingerprint`) beside the password
one and takes whichever finishes first. Upstream ships that for unlocking
only, never for login.

This is sddm/sddm#1220, which gives SDDM the same shape: a separate
`sddm-fingerprint` service driven by its own helper process, started when the
greeter connects, so the fingerprint and the password are two conversations
rather than one. Open since 2019 and unlikely to land -- SDDM is in
maintenance while KDE moves to Plasma Login Manager -- and packaged by others
in the meantime (AUR `sddm-fingerprint`).

Carried as-is except for the two files under services/: the PAM file it adds
is Arch-shaped (`system-local-login`, `pam_faillock`) and its install rule
does not apply to 0.21.0, which guards those installs with
INSTALL_PAM_CONFIGURATION. NixOS generates its PAM stacks from
`security.pam.services`, so both are dead weight here and the service is
defined in modules/greeter.nix instead.

Origin: https://github.com/sddm/sddm/pull/1220
